WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.800
The Legacy of the Wizard!

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:09.200
Long, long ago, an evil dragon terrorized the forest until it was imprisoned by a powerful wizard.

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:20.800
Now, years later, the dragon has revived and it's up to you to use the power of the wizard's descendants, the Drassel family, to defeat the dragon once again.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:44.360
NOSTALGIA And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:46.480
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:47.840
And I'm Joe.

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:50.480
Did you guys get all that at the beginning?

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:52.940
Like, was that not enough information?

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.740
Too much information? I think it was pretty good.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:03.480
I mean, that, this is one of my favorite, uh, this is one of my favorite instruction booklets for its, uh, story.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:09.160
Um, but you really did get most of, like, what, what makes it great.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:14.860
Yeah, it's quite a long back of the box compared to, uh, compared to most.

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:17.320
Because there's no, like, there's no stopping point.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.600
They just, it's like plot, plot, plot.

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:22.800
Like, they never let you, like, take a break and be like, oh, that, that's enough.

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:27.320
Like, that's not, that's enough to give the, uh, person an idea of what the game is.

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:30.340
It's like, you really have to read the whole back of the box to understand what you're playing.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:40.580
I, I actually think it's kind of funny that, or it's interesting that more games don't do that because they only, you know, the, the most common precedent to this would have been, like, books.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:43.820
And I feel like the back of books have, like, a whole long synopsis.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:45.760
But games don't usually do this.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:51.360
I mean, I mean, a lot of them are, have something like this going on, I think.

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:56.940
Uh, unless you mean specifically how much story is told in the back of the box.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:58.320
Yeah, that's what I, that's what I mean.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:02.920
I mean, usually there's like, it's usually like, break the boxes and defeat the evil lord.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:04.460
Whatever, do you remember that game?

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:05.500
Everyone remembers that game.

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:08.180
Yeah. Bowser is the evil lord?

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:17.180
Yeah, yeah. Um, also, legacy of the wizard, and yet you play as many characters.

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:21.380
Well, it's because you, you're all the legacy of the wizard.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:24.060
Like, you, uh, you are his spawn.

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:27.980
Yeah, like the wizard being the, the grandfather, right?

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:29.240
Whose picture's on the wall?

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:31.500
The one who's also just still standing there?

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:33.200
I was there. Yeah.

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:38.500
Uh, wait a minute. No, he's, he's, yeah, he's in prison, that, that wizard.

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:41.380
That, that, it's the legacy of that wizard, not your grandfather.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:45.060
I'm talking about the one long, long ago when the dragon was there.

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:47.980
The, the wizard, that wizard is the legacy.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:50.960
I could have sworn, the wizard is your grandfather.

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:55.340
Yeah, the, the, the, the grandmother tells the story and says, like, see that picture?

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:56.360
That is your grandfather.

00:02:57.380 --> 00:03:12.980
See, I didn't get, I guess I didn't get that from the back of the box, because I'm reading it and I'm thinking, like, well, that happened a long time ago, and now, like, you know, centuries later, the descendants of that wizard have to, like, you know, retame this dragon.

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:23.440
I, I get what you mean, because in the manual, like, uh, they start two paragraphs with a long, long time ago and long, long ago.

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:30.760
So, uh, but it's really just, like, you know, two generations, um, because at the end of that, they say that the wizard is your grandfather.

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:40.480
Um, and, uh, I think, uh, now he, I don't know who they're, like, she's speaking to, because your grandfather's there.

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:44.180
Like, he's, he's the one that, like, gives you passwords and stuff.

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:47.440
So, uh, I don't know if it is pretending he's not around.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:51.940
I don't know. Well, you can't play as him or grandma, really.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:53.420
They're just password boxes.

00:03:53.780 --> 00:04:00.400
But, uh, the rest of the family is completely playable, and I'll tell you, I didn't expect to play as a family in this game.

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:02.160
I'd say, I'd say the same.

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:07.720
Uh, it's, it's a, it's a neat little trick to give context to why there are multiple characters.

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:15.120
But, uh, yeah, playing as a, as a mom and then a daughter is, uh, it's, it's funny, funny to me.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:24.280
And this is the story of the Drassel family, as mentioned in the back of the box, and we're talking about this family a lot.

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:29.660
But did you know that Drassel, like that, is just an abbreviation for Dragon Slayer?

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:32.160
Do you guys know about the Dragon Slayer games?

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:35.100
I've heard of them.

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:37.160
I have not. Well.

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:41.860
It's a really lazy way to, um, make that the name of a family, though.

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:52.520
No, come on. Japanese people love doing that stuff where, like, they shorten, uh, you know, like, names of things to then bring them down to, to be like, that's the crazy reveal or something.

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:59.640
Obviously, it doesn't work here because they completely ignored the fact that this is a Dragon Slayer game because, I think this is the fourth Dragon Slayer game.

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:05.680
Uh, that would ever come out, but they're just like, nah, we'll just call it Legacy of the Wizard in America.

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:08.360
Hold on. This actually is a Dragon Slayer game, or are you?

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:09.740
This is a Dragon Slayer game.

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:10.880
I'm not joking at all.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:12.160
Oh, I didn't know that.

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:14.080
Yeah, and that's why they're called the Drassel family.

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:18.980
But in the, in the credits of the game, they're, they're given the last name Warzen.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:21.420
So, what happened to the Drassel family?

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:25.360
Warzen? Is that, like, answer backwards?

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:31.100
It's, you know, the, the, the Drassel family of House Warzen, or, you know, something like that.

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:34.720
I don't know what that's about, but I think that's, like, a funny, like, it's like a half commitment.

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:39.060
It's like, it is a Dragon Slayer game, but it's also not all in the same game.

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:40.080
My head hurts.

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:43.720
Sean, you mentioned the manual, and it is a good one.

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:47.920
The story of this game is, is mostly explained entirely in the manual.

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:53.320
There's very little, if anything, in the game to really give you any additional context.

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:58.640
So, did you feel like the manual was enough in terms of storytelling?

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:02.900
I never feel like a manual is enough in terms of storytelling.

00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:09.900
The only reason that I appreciate this one is because of how, how terribly written it is.

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:13.520
But, um, that doesn't mean it's any good.

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:24.420
Yeah, I, I feel like the only time I would say a manual would suffice as being the only means of storytelling is if, like, the story literally, like, just doesn't mean anything.

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:33.760
If it's just, like, a, if it's just, like, a random shmup that doesn't have much of a story, but then there's, like, a little bit of, like, oh, here's a little aside, a little extra thing in the manual if you want to read it.

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:38.960
But, like, this game seems like big, a big part of the adventure is the story of the adventure.

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:42.160
So, it would have been nice to have it, like, included in the manual.

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:47.860
Especially, like, today, I mean, if someone picks up this game, I mean, how many people Google the manual anymore?

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:52.160
You know, or, like, just, just, or even have the, you know, have the physical manual.

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:53.780
It would probably be a lot of people reading it.

00:06:54.520 --> 00:07:08.640
Yeah, and, I mean, you know, like, the game itself, even though it doesn't give you any context or anything, I guess, like, you know, the, the map itself is gigantic enough that you could just kind of build your own explanation for things and lore and stuff like that, right?

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:09.440
And that's half the fun.

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:11.680
It's, like, that's the fun of those Dark Souls games, right?

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:14.060
You just experience the lore and the world.

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:16.060
There's no, like, actual coherent story.

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:18.700
I guess you could say that.

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:20.960
It's a joke. I'm making a joke, but.

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:24.760
I mean, I was just trying to, trying to be nice about it.

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:31.120
Well, I also, though, do jokes aside, think that there is a, there is a happy medium between, depending on the kind of story.

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:36.820
Like, a Pokemon game, you know the story, you're trying to be a Pokemon trainer, but you sort of tell it as you go.

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:41.060
I guess it's not really that you're, you're making up the story and coming up with the story yourself, but it's, like.

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:45.520
No, but there's at least, like, exposition and, like, things that are said in the game.

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:48.220
Totally. But I do, I get what you're saying.

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:56.580
The game itself is a side-scrolling, I'm going to call it, like, platformer, but it's also kind of like a dungeon crawler, right?

00:07:56.580 --> 00:08:02.980
Because it's just this one giant dungeon that all takes place underground that you just kind of have to navigate yourself through.

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:08.380
I'm not quite sure what a dungeon crawler is, but would either of you back me up on that?

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:16.080
As someone who's also not quite sure what a dungeon crawler, specifically what you would define it as, yes, I'll back you up on that.

00:08:16.280 --> 00:08:17.900
It seems like you crawl through dungeons.

00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:24.900
I'd call a dungeon crawler something more similar to, like, almost like a Diablo.

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:32.020
So I wouldn't say that's what this is, but it's definitely a platform RPG hybrid.

00:08:33.180 --> 00:08:34.000
Right, right.

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:36.060
Okay, I'm happy with that description.

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:45.740
And, you know, we mentioned that you play as these six family members, and obviously they all have, like, unique traits and different characteristics that help explore this underground dungeon.

00:08:45.740 --> 00:08:52.860
But before we talk about the family members and all that stuff, pretend you're playing as just the most generic version of any of these characters.

00:08:53.400 --> 00:09:00.880
Let's talk about this gigantic underground cavern, because I feel like that's always what we're going to wind up going back to.

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:03.200
That is where most of the game takes place.

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:09.780
That is, like, the experience of being in that dungeon and trying to find your way around it.

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:20.400
So can I just get some thoughts, maybe Sean, we'll start with you, on just navigation and how, like, how exploration works in this giant underground cavern?

00:09:21.940 --> 00:09:42.940
Yeah, it's got, it's an interesting mix of, like, gameplay mechanics and level design here, where it has some similarities to, like, a Mario, and it has some similarities to, like, a top-down Zelda.

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:53.700
The, like, the orientation is very, it's very side-scroller, in that, like, up is, like, up and not north.

00:09:54.920 --> 00:10:04.340
But a lot of the mechanics seem to be designed for, like, an up-is-north kind of game, like, more of a top-down thing.

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:19.840
But basically, it's just a really big labyrinth, and you have to collect keys, you have to collect power-ups in order to interact with certain things, in order to get around and eventually slay this dragon.

00:10:22.360 --> 00:10:27.420
And, yeah, I think that's pretty much the basics of it.

00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:29.960
Absolutely. Joe, anything you want to add to that?

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:41.280
Yeah, so, I'll also say that I think they did a good job of aesthetically making, making it feel like there are different sections of this dungeon.

00:10:41.480 --> 00:10:49.960
So, like, I know when I'm, oh, I'm in that green section with all the ladders, and I'm in the section with all, you know, that looks really dark, and, you know, that, like, fiery red section and whatnot.

00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:52.620
And it makes it feel like you're progressing through.

00:10:52.620 --> 00:11:00.300
But I had this weird, like, back and forth of, like, whether I thought the areas felt unique or not.

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:04.300
And I think what I landed on is that the areas look unique.

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:16.600
But I think as far as, like, dungeon design, like, most of the game feels kind of the same, which I thought was a little bit annoying to me.

00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:26.640
Because there was such a big world, and there were so many different locations, but it always felt like you're doing the same thing, and there was not, like, too much that was unique as far as, like, gameplay.

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:35.900
Or, like, I don't know, it just all felt like sort of, if you were to skin it all similarly, it all felt like you're in, like, one place.

00:11:35.900 --> 00:11:36.760
Does that make any sense?

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:43.980
Yeah. Yeah, like, it's all coded so that you can know where you are in relation.

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:48.920
Like, if you are somebody that has made a map or is following a map.

00:11:49.060 --> 00:12:02.280
Like, this isn't like the game we played last week where most of the areas have sort of the same visual aesthetic and kind of makes everything look samey.

00:12:02.960 --> 00:12:12.620
In this, it's more like there's enough uniqueness to make it visually distinct for, like, a utilitarian purpose.

00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:23.580
But there isn't a lot of uniqueness in terms of how, like, to make it feel like a world and not just a maze.

00:12:24.040 --> 00:12:24.900
Yeah. Exactly.

00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:26.980
That's kind of what I wanted to go off there, Sean.

00:12:27.060 --> 00:12:34.020
That's a good point. You know, there's no real, like, aha moments early on or sense of discovery.

00:12:34.300 --> 00:12:40.780
You can go through a large amount of this maze without anything, like, spectacular happening to you.

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:45.780
Like, yeah, enemies will drop stuff and, you know, you might get, like, keys and things.

00:12:45.880 --> 00:12:47.320
And you're like, oh, I wonder what these keys do.

00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:53.280
But when you're playing another game that just kind of drops you in the middle and says, like, hey, go explore this stuff.

00:12:53.280 --> 00:12:54.920
I'm talking about the Legend of Zelda, right?

00:12:55.840 --> 00:13:00.020
It's not like you're, like, lost forever in the sense of what do I do now?

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:02.340
Like, okay, I got the sword from the old man.

00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:03.820
Like, now I can go anywhere on the map.

00:13:03.880 --> 00:13:09.080
It's like, no, you can kind of, like, go to this area and we're kind of guiding you towards this particular dungeon.

00:13:09.080 --> 00:13:11.300
But it's okay if you find one of the other two dungeons.

00:13:11.840 --> 00:13:23.800
In Legacy of the Wizard, there are really no clues and no real design rhythm to the map itself that, like, forces you to go to a particular thing that is, like, interesting.

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:27.640
And even if that was the case, it doesn't happen quick enough.

00:13:27.640 --> 00:13:37.280
You know, it just is one of those things where you could be going for a while, find something, and then it's like, oh, but actually I didn't bring the right family member.

00:13:37.540 --> 00:13:39.200
Like, this puzzle's not for them.

00:13:39.380 --> 00:13:42.480
And that was kind of like a, there's like a weird balance here.

00:13:42.700 --> 00:13:44.740
Is the map itself just too large?

00:13:46.300 --> 00:13:48.000
Yeah, and this is a huge map.

00:13:48.820 --> 00:13:50.880
I think it's bigger than Zelda.

00:13:52.120 --> 00:14:09.960
And, like, yeah, there isn't enough distinctness to it to make, like, because this game is designed for multiple playthroughs or runs or, like, whatever you would call a different run through with another family member.

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:18.340
But there really isn't a lot that makes me want to do this more than once, to go through these rooms more than once.

00:14:18.960 --> 00:14:22.760
Because once you know the trick, you know the trick, if there is one.

00:14:23.360 --> 00:14:35.880
And there's a lot that are just, like, so annoyingly laid out, to me at least, that there was a lot of times where I kind of just, like, sighed when I had to play through it again.

00:14:37.120 --> 00:14:39.300
So I think, yeah, that's where I come from on that.

00:14:39.820 --> 00:14:47.300
Yeah, I think that if you are going to make it this big, I don't think that's a problem as long as you make the areas feel distinct.

00:14:47.300 --> 00:14:51.740
Like, I want one area to be, you know, oh, this is where it's more puzzly.

00:14:51.740 --> 00:14:56.920
And this is where it's more, you know, where you've got to think about things this way, specifically in this area.

00:14:57.080 --> 00:14:58.360
Oh, and this area is a little harder.

00:14:58.860 --> 00:15:01.100
You know, the enemies, there's these interesting, unique enemies here.

00:15:01.300 --> 00:15:04.380
But just, like, everything felt, like, the same.

00:15:04.400 --> 00:15:06.700
And that just makes the map feel way too big.

00:15:07.720 --> 00:15:14.400
Yeah, this had my, like, my problem with the original Metroid that we went over in that episode long ago.

00:15:15.080 --> 00:15:36.080
It has it, like, amplified times 10 because, you know, in Metroid, I guess my complaint to that was that since there's no, like, general sense of direction and it's up to you to explore, like, that's half cool and half tedious of, like, trying to actually figure out where the item that you need is at any particular moment.

00:15:36.080 --> 00:15:39.780
Because the game's not pushing you in any sense.

00:15:39.960 --> 00:15:47.040
In this game, there's not even, like, the unlockability component because the keys are freely given out by enemies.

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:54.280
And the only way you find out, like, oh, I don't have the right thing is by finding out that you don't have the right character for the puzzle.

00:15:54.540 --> 00:16:01.900
And if you're going to do something like that, the switch out system should probably be, like, a lot simpler than what they proposed.

00:16:01.900 --> 00:16:04.860
Yeah, I agree. Yeah, they've got those ins throughout.

00:16:05.140 --> 00:16:07.220
And you can change your items in the ins.

00:16:07.500 --> 00:16:18.220
And I wish that you were able, you know, maybe it doesn't make narrative sense, but I wish that you were able to change your player at the ins rather than have to any time you want to switch, you have to go back to the beginning of the game.

00:16:18.560 --> 00:16:21.340
Gotta love backtracking for the sake of backtracking.

00:16:22.500 --> 00:16:24.160
So how much did you backtrack?

00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:26.840
How much were you like, all right, I will continue.

00:16:27.540 --> 00:16:28.940
I'm glad I found this thing.

00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:32.220
I'm going to go all the way back and do it with the correct person this time.

00:16:33.380 --> 00:16:49.220
I didn't have enough direction to know when I should do that because there are so many weird situations in the design of this catacomb that, like, just poking at walls or poking at ceilings, like, that'll be, like, these hidden areas.

00:16:49.360 --> 00:16:52.780
I didn't know when I was done with a certain character.

00:16:54.440 --> 00:16:58.220
It was just hard for me to tell, like, when I should go back.

00:16:58.220 --> 00:17:08.740
Yeah, I did a little bit of it because at first I just started out, just, I played as the dad first and then realized pretty quickly that I ran into, like, a block I couldn't pass.

00:17:08.820 --> 00:17:13.260
I knew I was from, I knew from the manual and from reading up on it a little bit, I needed to get the gloves.

00:17:13.560 --> 00:17:19.900
So then I pretty quickly went back and switched to the dog, quote unquote dog, who's, like, apparently not a dog.

00:17:20.100 --> 00:17:21.060
But, um.

00:17:21.240 --> 00:17:22.120
Hold on, hold on.

00:17:22.660 --> 00:17:30.320
The description of Pochi is, it acts like a dog, but it is really a monster.

00:17:30.560 --> 00:17:32.440
The other monsters don't bother Pochi.

00:17:33.380 --> 00:17:44.440
Yeah, which I thought that was actually kind of an interesting and a decent mechanic in the game where if you want to play as Pochi, you don't have to worry.

00:17:44.440 --> 00:17:45.660
Your enemies can't hurt you.

00:17:46.820 --> 00:17:55.780
Like, have we had a game that's given you, like, the option to just go through, like, the whole main portion of the map with invincibility, basically?

00:17:56.880 --> 00:18:04.680
Because it gave me, first of all, incentive to play first as Pochi and get whatever items I needed, namely the gloves.

00:18:04.680 --> 00:18:10.740
And I think I ended up getting something else with Pochi, but also, like, explore and get to know the map safely.

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:31.820
Yeah, I mean, I don't think I've, I don't think we've run into this specific thing, but I do like the idea of, like, these characters actually having, like, unique benefits that are more than just, like, more than just a tweaked stat, like a jump or power of an attack or something.

00:18:31.820 --> 00:18:36.600
So I thought that the implementation of Pochi was at least unique.

00:18:37.440 --> 00:18:44.880
Exactly, yeah. There should have been more Pochi, you know, like, there should have been more Pochis in this game.

00:18:45.080 --> 00:18:53.340
Not even in the sense of, like, more pets or anything like that, but just what they did with Pochi was really cool because it affects the entire dungeon, like Joe's talking about.

00:18:53.480 --> 00:18:55.780
That's like your, he's your scout, you know?

00:18:55.840 --> 00:18:59.260
You bring him down there and you figure out where to go and what's what.

00:18:59.260 --> 00:19:05.140
And then you go, okay, cool, I'll come back here with this character or, okay, I'll save this for later or don't go this way.

00:19:05.260 --> 00:19:08.260
It's a dead end, you know? Like, Pochi's really cool for figuring those things out.

00:19:08.500 --> 00:19:18.420
The other characters are really just used for, like, specific puzzle-solving moments, which doesn't feel as useful in the grand scheme of the dungeon.

00:19:18.420 --> 00:19:25.880
It'd be really cool if each character had elements that, like, affected the overall way that they go through the map.

00:19:26.020 --> 00:19:31.800
And I understand, like, yes, some of them have, like, different jump heights and, you know, different strengths and stuff like that.

00:19:31.920 --> 00:19:40.720
But I'm talking about, like, actually affecting the map itself in some way that makes it easier and harder for those specific characters to navigate.

00:19:41.400 --> 00:19:46.640
Yeah, I guess the other example would be that the father can move the blocks after using the gloves.

00:19:47.560 --> 00:19:54.540
I think that that's, like, a big enough switch to how you play the game for him to qualify.

00:19:55.140 --> 00:19:57.980
But the rest, yeah, they're just stat tweaks.

00:19:58.960 --> 00:20:07.600
Yeah, I feel like Pochi, the advantage you get from playing Pochi is so much more organic.

00:20:07.920 --> 00:20:12.200
Where it's just, like you said, it changes the game.

00:20:12.460 --> 00:20:17.180
Whereas, like, I think even the dad and the other ones where it's, like, you get the advantage from the dad of moving the blocks,

00:20:17.240 --> 00:20:20.320
but the developers placed those blocks in places, you know?

00:20:20.320 --> 00:20:25.300
So it feels like it's, like, well, we put this obstacle here so you can have someone that has to get through it.

00:20:25.340 --> 00:20:30.060
It's more constructed. Yeah, which is totally a valid, you know, game design choice.

00:20:30.060 --> 00:20:34.900
But it's just, it just doesn't feel quite as significant as the Pochi thing.

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:39.460
And then even, you know, some characters where it's, like, oh, this person's, yeah, I think with the daughter,

00:20:39.460 --> 00:20:43.540
you can jump on enemies and, like, there's other things that help you dispatch enemies more easily.

00:20:45.300 --> 00:20:49.540
But did we really ever have too much trouble fighting the enemies in the first place in this game?

00:20:49.540 --> 00:20:53.100
Yeah. Which almost invalidates my whole Pochi.

00:20:53.200 --> 00:20:54.580
Because of how many of them were there.

00:20:55.360 --> 00:21:01.040
Right. It'd be cool if, not to harp on this subject, but since you were suggesting stuff like that, Joe,

00:21:01.180 --> 00:21:05.400
it'd be cool if, like, instead of the dad's ability to, like, move blocks,

00:21:05.400 --> 00:21:11.580
if he got, if he was, like, a digger instead and he could, like, dig new holes to get to, you know,

00:21:11.640 --> 00:21:17.580
create new access points between the map that, like, another character could use, you know,

00:21:17.580 --> 00:21:21.700
now that you've made that whole, like, I mean, that's not possible probably in an NES game.

00:21:21.740 --> 00:21:24.960
And I'm really, like, leaning on my SteamWorld dig knowledge here.

00:21:25.340 --> 00:21:31.300
But I think something like that would also be a cool way of affecting the maze through character powers,

00:21:31.420 --> 00:21:37.840
you know, as opposed to just being like, well, now he's the only one who can solve this puzzle thanks to his gloves.

00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:42.280
Right. Well, I guess also the mom has, because she can fly, right?

00:21:42.360 --> 00:21:47.040
So she can just go in 3D space anywhere on the map.

00:21:47.040 --> 00:21:49.180
I think it takes away magic to do that.

00:21:49.320 --> 00:21:52.020
But that's, like, a pretty significant thing.

00:21:52.740 --> 00:21:54.140
She can go in 3D space?

00:21:55.320 --> 00:21:58.160
Well, you can go, I guess, 2D.

00:21:58.280 --> 00:22:01.000
You can go up, yeah. But you know what I mean.

00:22:01.380 --> 00:22:09.340
Yeah. On top of all of the hard-to-navigate sections of the game, you know, there's dead ends.

00:22:10.080 --> 00:22:11.820
There's pits that you can fall into.

00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:14.700
They're not like pits of death, though, right?

00:22:14.800 --> 00:22:16.640
I mean, they will do damage to you.

00:22:16.740 --> 00:22:20.320
But they just lead to new areas, which then you can't get back to the previous areas.

00:22:20.520 --> 00:22:24.780
And then there's also, like, hidden areas that you're supposed to kind of organically find.

00:22:24.780 --> 00:22:29.500
But, you know, sometimes it's just like, well, that's a block and it should block my path.

00:22:29.620 --> 00:22:31.560
But I can actually, turns out, I can walk right through it.

00:22:31.700 --> 00:22:34.020
Like, it's hard to know these things are happening.

00:22:34.660 --> 00:22:41.560
And I feel like all of these things sound like cool ideas if they were used, you know, like, as one-offs, right?

00:22:41.680 --> 00:22:45.060
Like, it'd be cool if there was a dead end once, right?

00:22:45.060 --> 00:22:46.480
Like, oh, you went the wrong way.

00:22:46.620 --> 00:22:47.880
Like, cool.

00:22:48.120 --> 00:22:53.000
I'm done with that. No, it's like, you know, programming that stuff, the pits and the hidden areas.

00:22:53.000 --> 00:22:57.740
Like, programming that stuff frequently throughout the map then just becomes, like, a thing you check for now all the time.

00:22:58.440 --> 00:23:05.340
Yeah, it ended up being that the way I'd navigate this game was just pushing up against every wall, every ceiling that I could find.

00:23:05.380 --> 00:23:08.020
And I don't like doing that.

00:23:09.880 --> 00:23:12.960
It's sort of, like, the anti-fun for me.

00:23:14.240 --> 00:23:19.740
But, I mean, it makes it a much more challenging puzzle or a much more challenging maze.

00:23:20.540 --> 00:23:25.880
Yeah, this is something that bothered me a lot because it's just something we see so often on the NES.

00:23:26.080 --> 00:23:38.360
And, like, I don't really understand why there's so many, like, just completely invisible paths that are necessary to complete games like back then.

00:23:38.360 --> 00:23:46.480
And it just, it makes me, you know, feel like I would never want to try and beat this game without a guide.

00:23:46.700 --> 00:23:53.440
And I hate having to, like, look for after every step, check the guide and make sure I've gone the right way.

00:23:53.580 --> 00:23:56.880
You know, like, that's, I think, the way that guides suck.

00:23:57.100 --> 00:24:02.760
You know, there's, like, a way that a guide can be used where it's like, oh, well, this guide is a nice complement to it.

00:24:02.760 --> 00:24:05.460
But other times it's just, like, why am I even playing this game?

00:24:05.480 --> 00:24:07.980
It feels like a chore to just be, like, following instructions.

00:24:09.060 --> 00:24:19.220
Yeah. Yeah, I think, like, they have an idea of selling this game as, like, you know, oh, but you'll go, like, out to recess and talk with the other kids who are also playing this game.

00:24:19.380 --> 00:24:23.420
And you'll share, like, you know, where you found your hidden walls and stuff like that.

00:24:23.540 --> 00:24:28.460
And that's a cute picture for people who want to believe that that happened to them in the 80s, you know.

00:24:28.740 --> 00:24:30.140
That wants to believe.

00:24:30.140 --> 00:24:34.160
Right, you know, again, they just think that, like, they always discussed secrets.

00:24:34.340 --> 00:24:39.500
Like, try describing a secret that you found in this game to another kid without a map, you know.

00:24:39.620 --> 00:24:43.760
Like, just try being, like, so I was in this room and it was pink and then there was this blue block.

00:24:43.760 --> 00:24:46.880
But turns out it's not, you know, it's not actually a block.

00:24:46.880 --> 00:24:47.740
You can go through it.

00:24:48.020 --> 00:24:49.920
Like, no one's going to know what you're talking about.

00:24:50.280 --> 00:24:55.800
So, in reality, the real thing of the game is, like Joe was mentioning, is, like, it's to call the Nintendo Power Hotline.

00:24:55.800 --> 00:24:58.220
It's to buy the strategy guide.

00:24:58.420 --> 00:25:00.140
It's to do, you know, it's to do stuff like that.

00:25:00.300 --> 00:25:06.120
These games add these extra elements that essentially become, as Sean's mentioning, they become, like, checklists.

00:25:06.160 --> 00:25:08.920
Like, go in every room and make sure you push up against the walls.

00:25:09.060 --> 00:25:10.680
Did I find the secret one?

00:25:11.900 --> 00:25:18.120
Yeah. One other thing that I want to mention about this game is, like, the moment-to-moment gameplay.

00:25:18.560 --> 00:25:32.540
This, the design of the labyrinth does not seem to be coherent or, like, copacetic with the, like, physics in this game.

00:25:34.040 --> 00:25:47.180
Like, your jump and the, like, how precise your movements are don't seem to really mesh well with how tight a lot of these corridors and tight these jumps are.

00:25:47.400 --> 00:25:59.460
It seems a lot of the times that, like, in order to get into parts of this map, I'm trying to, like, wrestle with the game itself to, like, get into this little hole.

00:25:59.460 --> 00:26:06.900
It seems, like, very not how it was designed, and I'm almost, like, going to where I'm not supposed to be, but that's sort of just how it is.

00:26:07.040 --> 00:26:08.800
Did you guys feel this way when you were playing?

00:26:09.880 --> 00:26:14.240
Yeah, specifically when you're jumping into a one-tile space.

00:26:14.680 --> 00:26:15.900
Yeah. Yeah, I was getting that a lot.

00:26:16.100 --> 00:26:25.640
It has to come down with the fact that they designed everybody to have, like, a somewhat different height jump, but then they didn't, you know, like,

00:26:25.640 --> 00:26:30.200
they didn't account for all of those different types of characters going through this maze, you know?

00:26:30.320 --> 00:26:38.680
It seems like, I get that, like, you know, some of the puzzle portion is, like, well, make sure you have somebody who can jump really high for this or not, you know?

00:26:38.680 --> 00:26:49.280
But it seems like, to your point, Sean, about not being intuitive, I feel like when I had the, I think the daughter is the highest jumper.

00:26:49.800 --> 00:26:56.120
Yeah. And when you play as her, she just feels, it's like, it should feel floaty, right?

00:26:56.140 --> 00:27:06.780
You should feel like you're able to, like, jump and land wherever you want to and stuff like that, but instead, you're, like, hitting ceilings or hitting platforms that you shouldn't be having in the way, you know?

00:27:06.780 --> 00:27:10.140
Well, and then there's the weird implementation of fall damage.

00:27:10.360 --> 00:27:19.800
Like, this game, in order to, like, actually maneuver around it, and maybe this is, like, oh, you're supposed to just play as the mom who can fly her way down.

00:27:19.960 --> 00:27:27.260
But, like, there are so many parts of this game where, like, in order to get to the next screen, you just have to fall, like, three screens down.

00:27:27.660 --> 00:27:31.800
And it's not a lot, but you do take damage when that happens.

00:27:31.800 --> 00:27:44.180
And even more bizarrely, it's got a very short amount of vertical distance that you have to clear in order to, like, get that fall damage.

00:27:44.280 --> 00:27:47.940
So that also felt really wacky to me.

00:27:48.080 --> 00:27:54.360
I looked into it, and the fall damage is in response to the highest the character can jump.

00:27:54.360 --> 00:28:02.600
So if you fall further than the highest point that the character can jump in tile spaces, you know, then, like, it counts as fall damage.

00:28:02.760 --> 00:28:07.200
And to your point, for most characters, that's going to be fall damage most of the time.

00:28:07.820 --> 00:28:16.320
Yeah. But did you guys ever, how often did you guys feel in danger of losing all your life?

00:28:16.640 --> 00:28:21.740
Like, there was a point, a period of time where I just started to realize, like, I'll just take the fall damage.

00:28:22.380 --> 00:28:25.800
I have no idea how much life I have.

00:28:25.900 --> 00:28:29.040
Was I missing something, or is there no way to tell?

00:28:29.240 --> 00:28:30.600
There's absolutely a way to tell.

00:28:30.720 --> 00:28:32.080
I mean, there's a life bar.

00:28:32.240 --> 00:28:36.860
I'm pretty sure. But it's filled up several times.

00:28:37.400 --> 00:28:39.380
So your life bar fills up all just...

00:28:39.380 --> 00:28:47.540
Yeah, it's like each top bar is one of, is like a full, each top, like, little unit is a full bar on the bottom.

00:28:48.000 --> 00:28:53.860
But the amount of damage that any given enemy doles out seems to be kind of random.

00:28:54.140 --> 00:29:01.400
And I think that a lot of that randomness comes from, like, that this game doesn't really have iframes that I could tell.

00:29:01.400 --> 00:29:07.520
So just sort of intersecting with an enemy for a little too long will give you multiple chunks.

00:29:08.420 --> 00:29:10.380
Well, I feel really dumb.

00:29:10.520 --> 00:29:17.820
I did not realize that, like, each red peg, I'm looking at it right now, each red peg clearly is, like, how many blue bars you have.

00:29:18.000 --> 00:29:19.960
Oh, yeah. So that actually tells you your point of thing.

00:29:19.960 --> 00:29:21.060
That's how the magic works. That's how the gold works.

00:29:21.320 --> 00:29:22.280
But, yeah, I didn't even...

00:29:22.280 --> 00:29:23.980
I just thought that was just two bars of health.

00:29:24.200 --> 00:29:27.780
I don't know why I didn't, like, wonder why one was red and one was blue.

00:29:27.920 --> 00:29:29.280
That's actually so obvious now.

00:29:29.280 --> 00:29:31.380
I feel stupid.

00:29:31.440 --> 00:29:36.020
We'll edit that out. No, the red one is your blood and the blue one is your soul.

00:29:36.320 --> 00:29:40.680
And it's like, you know, you have to keep your vitality and your soul alive.

00:29:42.480 --> 00:29:44.160
That's not true. Don't play the game that way.

00:29:44.760 --> 00:29:48.000
See, it's funny that I didn't get this because it's actually very Kingdom Hearts.

00:29:48.260 --> 00:29:49.500
Like, Kingdom Hearts 2 onwards.

00:29:49.880 --> 00:29:52.240
Kind of how, like, enemy health bars sort of look.

00:29:53.400 --> 00:29:56.160
Yeah. Bring it home to the KH.

00:29:56.560 --> 00:29:57.700
Gotta bring it up.

00:29:58.380 --> 00:30:02.680
Joe, I feel like what you want to talk about next is the combat.

00:30:03.000 --> 00:30:05.800
You've been teasing that it's kind of easy and dull.

00:30:06.640 --> 00:30:11.840
And, you know, the character that you pick is supposed to play a role in the combat of this game, right?

00:30:11.960 --> 00:30:13.740
They have different stats and stuff like that.

00:30:13.940 --> 00:30:15.300
Did you feel like that at all?

00:30:15.400 --> 00:30:17.800
Like, they all have projectile weapons, right?

00:30:17.860 --> 00:30:19.600
So at the end of the day, everybody does...

00:30:19.600 --> 00:30:20.580
Except for Pochi, right?

00:30:20.640 --> 00:30:22.980
He doesn't... Because he doesn't have to take damage anyway.

00:30:23.280 --> 00:30:24.460
But... He just walks around.

00:30:24.640 --> 00:30:25.960
Right. But the other characters...

00:30:25.960 --> 00:30:27.620
Wait, no. He does have a weapon.

00:30:27.820 --> 00:30:29.400
He has, like, a melee attack, though, right?

00:30:29.440 --> 00:30:31.620
It's like a... Oh, is that only a one-frame thing?

00:30:31.780 --> 00:30:34.320
But I think that also the father's...

00:30:34.320 --> 00:30:36.100
I mean, I guess it's technically projectile.

00:30:36.240 --> 00:30:39.120
It goes like a tile ahead of you and then disappears.

00:30:40.400 --> 00:30:44.860
But it's not like some of the projectile weapons you see later with other characters.

00:30:45.760 --> 00:30:47.920
But the enemies aren't necessarily menacing.

00:30:48.360 --> 00:30:51.300
They, you know, like, they will charge at you, I suppose.

00:30:51.300 --> 00:30:53.000
But they don't really, like, attack.

00:30:53.380 --> 00:30:54.480
It feels kind of weird.

00:30:54.600 --> 00:30:56.660
It feels like we're intruding on their land.

00:30:58.020 --> 00:31:10.340
And, you know, the combat system that they have in place here is also weird because you don't know how sometimes how many times it's going to take to hit a certain person to kill them off.

00:31:10.340 --> 00:31:17.900
Because even same enemy class types will take, like, this one took three, but the next one will only take one hit.

00:31:17.900 --> 00:31:19.780
And then the next one's going to take two hits.

00:31:19.860 --> 00:31:20.960
It's like it always felt random.

00:31:21.280 --> 00:31:22.820
Did you guys experience that at all?

00:31:25.080 --> 00:31:27.080
Yeah, a little bit. I mean, I never really knew.

00:31:27.220 --> 00:31:33.120
I never really knew, I guess, or, like, was able to keep enough track to, like, be like, oh, these are the guys that take three hits.

00:31:33.180 --> 00:31:34.140
These are the guys that take five.

00:31:34.340 --> 00:31:36.700
So maybe, I mean, it's possible I just didn't notice.

00:31:37.040 --> 00:31:43.120
But also I think that that also speaks to how, like, yeah, the enemies, some of them are designed differently.

00:31:43.240 --> 00:31:48.080
But similar to the rooms, like, none of them do much that's that different.

00:31:48.440 --> 00:31:52.600
There's nothing, there's not many that you have to think about a different way of how to take on this enemy.

00:31:52.740 --> 00:31:54.620
Like, oh, that one might be a little faster than that guy.

00:31:54.780 --> 00:31:56.240
And this guy goes in a different direction.

00:31:56.580 --> 00:31:59.520
But, like, for the most part, they all just kind of walk back and forth.

00:31:59.520 --> 00:32:02.980
Yeah, you really just have to pay attention to, like, the different archetypes.

00:32:03.140 --> 00:32:08.340
Like, this is going to be one that will just patrol a certain platform.

00:32:08.460 --> 00:32:15.180
This is one that is sort of, like, removed from the constraints of the map and can go anywhere.

00:32:15.660 --> 00:32:20.040
There are the ones that are, like, they'll go into, like, a hot pursuit if you end up being close.

00:32:20.040 --> 00:32:35.220
But that, like, those, like, five or whatever kinds of enemies end up covering, like, 50, maybe not 50, like, a dozen or more different, like, unique sprites.

00:32:35.920 --> 00:32:36.960
Unique enemies.

00:32:37.300 --> 00:32:39.400
So, yeah, it was hard for me to tell.

00:32:39.740 --> 00:32:45.640
Maybe the other difference is how much damage they make or how much damage they can take.

00:32:46.340 --> 00:32:55.280
But, like, if you take that and, like, think about now, like, the simplest Mario level you can think of, you're probably going to have Goombas, which you just jump on regularly.

00:32:55.420 --> 00:33:00.480
You're going to have Koopa Troopas, which you've got to hit twice, and you kick the turtle shell, and that makes you think about them a little differently.

00:33:00.480 --> 00:33:03.760
You're going to have the fire flowers that shoot fireballs at you.

00:33:04.080 --> 00:33:08.680
And there's just different ways of thinking about there's reasons for these enemies to be different.

00:33:09.040 --> 00:33:15.200
We're here, like, I'm just watching right now, and, like, all the enemies I'm seeing on screen in this video I'm watching are just walking back and forth.

00:33:15.200 --> 00:33:16.360
There's one that's, like, hopping.

00:33:17.560 --> 00:33:19.440
But, like, you don't have to deal with them any differently.

00:33:19.640 --> 00:33:20.880
You're right. I forgot about that kind.

00:33:22.180 --> 00:33:28.020
But it makes me wish that there was, you know, like, maybe less enemies, right?

00:33:28.100 --> 00:33:33.520
And it was more about navigating and exploring and solving these puzzles, but also the maze itself.

00:33:33.520 --> 00:33:43.440
And then there were, like, sections where, like, you know, oh, this room's filled with, like, meaningful enemies that do some damage, so make sure you bring, like, your strongest character.

00:33:43.820 --> 00:33:48.580
Or, you know, this is a, you know, because I think there's a couple bosses in the game, too.

00:33:48.760 --> 00:33:56.240
But it'd be cool if, like, some rooms were just, like, small mini boss fights with, like, a particular enemy that takes, like, a bunch of shots to kill.

00:33:56.240 --> 00:33:59.100
And, you know, I guess I'm describing a boss fight, so I just want more boss fights.

00:33:59.240 --> 00:34:00.480
But, like, you know what I mean?

00:34:00.480 --> 00:34:09.480
Like, I think at the end of the day, the enemies that they lay out throughout every single stage just become another part of the tedious thing that you have to do in each room.

00:34:09.480 --> 00:34:14.520
Like, check the walls, check where, you know, like, if you have the correct character.

00:34:14.880 --> 00:34:18.300
And, oh, please take care of all these enemies, even though they pose no threat to you.

00:34:19.520 --> 00:34:27.340
Yeah, and I guess another thing that they do, like, some of the enemies can be used as platforms, which can be used in the puzzle-solving element of it.

00:34:27.440 --> 00:34:33.740
But they just seem to be so randomly placed that it doesn't really matter that much.

00:34:33.740 --> 00:34:45.700
Like, yeah, you're going to find the ones that you probably need somewhere in the vicinity of the puzzle, but it doesn't feel as, like, as polished as it could have.

00:35:10.240 --> 00:35:15.440
One thing I wanted to mention about, can we talk about the pickups just a little bit?

00:35:15.640 --> 00:35:17.220
Yeah, totally. We're good for that.

00:35:17.840 --> 00:35:25.860
Cool. This is just a skin-deep sort of thing, but the pickup that gives you more health just looks like a pair of butt cheeks.

00:35:27.200 --> 00:35:28.680
That's all I wanted to say on it.

00:35:28.820 --> 00:35:29.880
Thank you for that.

00:35:30.280 --> 00:35:31.100
Yeah, I agree.

00:35:31.300 --> 00:35:33.060
I totally agree. Yeah?

00:35:33.060 --> 00:35:34.400
That was my first thought.

00:35:35.180 --> 00:35:36.900
Was it really, or you were just making...

00:35:36.900 --> 00:35:37.800
No, I really do agree.

00:35:37.940 --> 00:35:42.540
You know I sound like I'm being, like, dickishly sarcastic, but I really was being serious.

00:35:43.680 --> 00:35:58.260
Okay, good. Yeah, I like the idea of the, like, of the items and the way that, like, you know, certain characters use different items and you have to, like...

00:35:58.860 --> 00:36:02.300
If you plan it out properly, you can...

00:36:02.300 --> 00:36:07.920
Or if you look at a guide like I did, you can, you know, make it work out in a way where it's like you get...

00:36:07.920 --> 00:36:12.740
You play as the dog and you get all the items that you need for the father.

00:36:12.920 --> 00:36:18.100
So then you can switch to the father and, like, complete pretty much his journey with the items that you need.

00:36:18.100 --> 00:36:20.600
And while you're doing that, get the items you need for the mother.

00:36:20.800 --> 00:36:24.160
And then you grab, use those items in, like, in different ways.

00:36:24.200 --> 00:36:25.740
And it gives you...

00:36:26.260 --> 00:36:32.240
If you're using a guide, you can just go through and, like, optimize what you're doing.

00:36:32.240 --> 00:36:36.280
And if you're not using a guide, it was a good...

00:36:36.280 --> 00:36:43.920
At least a good attempt at, like, giving you this feeling of, like, discovery where it's like, oh, like, now I've got this thing.

00:36:43.980 --> 00:36:46.880
I wonder if I can go back and play as the father.

00:36:47.240 --> 00:36:53.840
And, like, to bring it back to another game I bring up all the time, Ratchet & Clank, that was one of the things I loved about the original Ratchet & Clank.

00:36:53.900 --> 00:36:55.460
Where it was like, I felt like I could...

00:36:55.460 --> 00:36:59.760
I go to this planet and that, like, suddenly opens up something on this other planet I've already been to.

00:36:59.760 --> 00:37:01.920
And I can go back there and start doing something.

00:37:02.080 --> 00:37:04.720
And I get this item that I'm like, oh, this will unlock something on this other planet.

00:37:04.740 --> 00:37:06.940
And it's a lot of backtracking in, like, a good way.

00:37:07.600 --> 00:37:10.960
And I felt like this could have scratched that itch.

00:37:11.880 --> 00:37:19.260
I do think that it was a little bit lacking in how you use these items that, like, didn't make it, you know, quite as unique as I liked.

00:37:19.320 --> 00:37:27.380
But I really did appreciate that little bit of, like, thought into why you would play this character first and then go back to play another character.

00:37:28.380 --> 00:37:48.740
Yeah. All right, so let's talk about the characters because we've been, you know, mentioning their usefulness and stuff like that.

00:37:48.740 --> 00:37:55.180
But actually, like, you know, the six characters and the uniqueness of them, compare it to, say, Super Mario Bros.

00:37:55.300 --> 00:37:56.180
2, right?

00:37:56.300 --> 00:38:00.160
Where you could pick between Mario, Luigi, Toad, and Peach, right?

00:38:00.220 --> 00:38:01.220
Or Princess Toadstool.

00:38:02.420 --> 00:38:03.320
And that's it.

00:38:03.400 --> 00:38:05.220
Like, you're locked to them for that particular level.

00:38:05.220 --> 00:38:08.080
But they all clearly can clear the stage.

00:38:08.180 --> 00:38:11.020
And they have, like, this one cool thing that they do.

00:38:11.600 --> 00:38:13.860
Did it feel like that with the six characters?

00:38:13.860 --> 00:38:16.180
Like, obviously, there's no, like, levels or anything like that.

00:38:16.260 --> 00:38:24.120
But did it feel like there was enough uniqueness between the six of them that you'd be like, oh, well, maybe I'd like to do another playthrough where I start as the sun.

00:38:24.120 --> 00:38:27.280
Or is that just, like, not a realistic expectation here?

00:38:29.040 --> 00:38:30.340
For me, it's not.

00:38:30.340 --> 00:38:38.640
But I can see how somebody playing this game in its time might think that.

00:38:40.680 --> 00:38:46.400
That's, like, not a – it's obviously not, like, a new concept in, like, replayability in games.

00:38:49.180 --> 00:38:58.360
But I think that the first playthrough has to be – has to have a few less frustrations for me personally for me to want to do that.

00:39:00.400 --> 00:39:03.900
Yeah, and I think as far as the comparison to Super Mario Bros.

00:39:04.060 --> 00:39:08.400
2 goes, I feel like they were trying to do such very different things.

00:39:08.600 --> 00:39:10.540
Like you said, Mike, in Super Mario Bros.

00:39:10.620 --> 00:39:14.740
2, you could beat any stage with any player – with any character, rather.

00:39:16.220 --> 00:39:22.380
Whereas this, like, the whole point seemed to be, like, you need to use certain characters for certain things.

00:39:22.380 --> 00:39:27.340
So you have to, like, figure out their strengths and figure out where one can get that others can't.

00:39:29.020 --> 00:39:32.480
But, like, so, like – so, no, I don't feel like it felt the same way as Super Mario Bros.

00:39:32.560 --> 00:39:34.780
2. But I also don't necessarily think it was trying to.

00:39:36.780 --> 00:39:41.500
But if we're just looking at them on, like, the face value of, like, hey, they each have different abilities.

00:39:41.700 --> 00:39:45.220
I think that, like, the different abilities felt a little better in Super Mario Bros.

00:39:45.340 --> 00:39:46.480
2 than here.

00:39:47.740 --> 00:39:52.000
But, I mean, you know, honestly, I'm already, like, not sure that I fully believe that.

00:39:52.000 --> 00:39:55.220
I mean, there's some interesting – like, we were talking about the dog and this.

00:39:55.300 --> 00:39:56.360
Like, there are some interesting things.

00:39:56.360 --> 00:39:58.980
It's just maybe not surrounded by as good of a game.

00:39:59.280 --> 00:40:03.620
I think what it came down to for me, though, too, is, like – and we talked about this briefly.

00:40:04.420 --> 00:40:12.240
When you find out that you don't have the right character, there's, like, almost a moment where you realize, like,

00:40:12.240 --> 00:40:18.100
like, oh, I have to go back and get the other character that just makes you kind of want to – at least me –

00:40:18.100 --> 00:40:19.940
made me kind of want to, like, put down the game.

00:40:20.080 --> 00:40:22.660
It's, like, not even if it was a good game, bad game, whatever.

00:40:22.880 --> 00:40:29.100
Just, like, the fact that you would have to backtrack all your progress and, like, do everything you just did again

00:40:29.100 --> 00:40:33.020
just to get to the next character and do it all again with them.

00:40:33.020 --> 00:40:35.500
But now, like, you figured out that you have the right thing.

00:40:35.720 --> 00:40:39.560
And knowing that it was just going to be a matter of time before you get to another scenario where it's, like,

00:40:39.560 --> 00:40:42.480
even though you swapped that time, now, oh, you need another new character.

00:40:43.200 --> 00:40:52.020
Something about that loop isn't – you know, I just don't even know if, like, they've tested that enough to figure out, like,

00:40:52.120 --> 00:40:55.280
hey, this is – we're asking the players to do a lot here, you know?

00:40:55.420 --> 00:41:04.800
Like, it seems like there should have been another way to meaningfully take the uniqueness of the characters

00:41:05.280 --> 00:41:15.020
and make it matter in the dungeon more than just, like, exceedingly, you know, like, larger and larger as you continue to explore the map.

00:41:15.180 --> 00:41:19.260
Exceedingly larger and larger amounts of time backtracking as you continue to go further down.

00:41:19.440 --> 00:41:25.300
Yeah, that's sort of why I had that, like, train of thought when, like, when we were talking about, like,

00:41:25.320 --> 00:41:26.780
when do you know when to go back?

00:41:26.900 --> 00:41:28.380
Like, yeah, you've got this pickup now.

00:41:29.160 --> 00:41:31.260
Like, do you go back or do you continue to search?

00:41:31.560 --> 00:41:42.120
I think that may have, like, colored my instinct to just keep trying because I really didn't want to go back and restart everything.

00:41:44.380 --> 00:41:46.660
So I'm in the same boat as you with that.

00:41:46.840 --> 00:41:49.040
Yeah, I mean, I completely agree also.

00:41:49.220 --> 00:41:53.740
And I think that, like, a way that they could have done it that would have been more bearable to me,

00:41:53.740 --> 00:42:00.560
much more bearable to me, is if it was designed in such a way that, like, there's always something for me to do at that end

00:42:00.560 --> 00:42:07.400
with the character I've chosen or, like, beyond that, you know, maybe I see the door that I can't get through without the dad.

00:42:07.580 --> 00:42:11.480
But while I'm playing as the, you know, as the daughter, I'm like, okay, I see that.

00:42:11.620 --> 00:42:16.700
I can move on and still go do something that I need to get done and then remember to come back here.

00:42:16.700 --> 00:42:23.920
And, like, there'd probably have to be some interesting stuff along the way that'll be different playing as the father than the daughter

00:42:23.920 --> 00:42:26.200
that'll still make me want to come back and do it all again.

00:42:26.320 --> 00:42:33.180
But, like, to just hit that dead end and then realize, like, realize, like, oh, shit, like, that was all for nothing.

00:42:33.180 --> 00:42:36.760
I had to come back and do it all again with another character is kind of a spirit breaker.

00:42:38.100 --> 00:42:40.960
Joe? Uh-oh, did you lose me?

00:42:41.360 --> 00:42:42.480
You are the father.

00:42:43.160 --> 00:42:44.900
Oh. Whoa.

00:42:46.120 --> 00:42:48.420
I thought you lost connection with me or something.

00:42:48.540 --> 00:42:49.960
You're like, Joe, right after my...

00:42:49.960 --> 00:42:54.280
Sorry. He lost connection with you, his father.

00:42:54.820 --> 00:43:01.200
Right, right. There are four boss fights and then, I guess, like, a final boss fight.

00:43:01.200 --> 00:43:02.020
So there's five boss fights.

00:43:02.120 --> 00:43:03.840
I have a tough time talking about boss fights today.

00:43:04.340 --> 00:43:09.340
And it's only going to continue because I don't really know how to talk about these boss fights

00:43:09.340 --> 00:43:14.620
except for that, like, all the complaints we had about the regular enemies no longer matter

00:43:14.620 --> 00:43:18.120
because your combat now sucks compared to the bosses.

00:43:18.420 --> 00:43:24.060
Like, the bosses are really hard in this game and they're just doing so much more damage and everything.

00:43:24.120 --> 00:43:26.840
They're just doing so much more of everything than the enemies were.

00:43:26.840 --> 00:43:29.520
And it's kind of like, hey, what the heck?

00:43:29.700 --> 00:43:31.920
Like, you know, first it was too easy.

00:43:32.100 --> 00:43:33.020
Now it's too hard.

00:43:33.280 --> 00:43:34.780
I'm not having a good time, man.

00:43:35.420 --> 00:43:41.940
Yeah, there's way too much of a discrepancy between, like, standard combat and boss combat.

00:43:43.360 --> 00:43:51.900
Like, usually I would think that a boss is sort of like what you've been doing throughout the game.

00:43:51.900 --> 00:43:59.320
It's a challenge to, like, it's challenging your skills that you've been playing with, like,

00:43:59.400 --> 00:44:02.700
at a smaller scope throughout the game.

00:44:02.700 --> 00:44:05.940
And it's just like a completely different game in that way.

00:44:07.200 --> 00:44:10.820
I, so I admittedly only faced, I think, two of the bosses.

00:44:17.360 --> 00:44:21.920
Like, first of all, there was no design of, like, oh, you've got to recognize their pattern or whatever.

00:44:22.020 --> 00:44:25.720
It was just hit them more times than they hit you as quickly as possible.

00:44:25.980 --> 00:44:28.080
Yeah, but you didn't find yourself taking a lot of damage?

00:44:28.740 --> 00:44:33.580
I found myself taking a lot of damage, but I always found, I mean, so both times I fought the bosses,

00:44:33.580 --> 00:44:37.040
I went to an inn first and got my life back.

00:44:37.160 --> 00:44:42.700
And then I just went in there and just rammed the attack button and just watched both of our life drain,

00:44:42.840 --> 00:44:44.820
but the boss drained before mine.

00:44:45.440 --> 00:44:46.260
It's a great strategy.

00:44:46.360 --> 00:44:51.340
So I'm actually surprised to hear you guys say that, but maybe it's because I healed beforehand that it was...

00:44:51.340 --> 00:44:54.220
I'm thinking that has to be what it is because I didn't heal beforehand.

00:44:55.180 --> 00:44:57.560
But obviously, like, that would be the correct strategy.

00:44:58.180 --> 00:44:59.040
I just think... Yeah, that's our fault.

00:44:59.040 --> 00:45:02.440
So... Yeah. You know, at the end of the day, I guess I just...

00:45:02.440 --> 00:45:04.600
It's not that they're, like, impossible boss fights.

00:45:04.700 --> 00:45:09.000
I just think it's funny how, like, all the other enemies in this game are so inconsequential

00:45:09.000 --> 00:45:11.540
and, like, the worst thing that can happen is they just run into you.

00:45:11.660 --> 00:45:14.840
But then the boss fights come out and, like Sean was saying,

00:45:15.020 --> 00:45:19.180
they just kind of remembered that there should be boss fights in this game.

00:45:19.580 --> 00:45:22.440
You know, like, they're not really teaching you anything about the, you know,

00:45:22.500 --> 00:45:26.540
like, new tricks you've learned or specific to the environments you've been dealing with

00:45:26.540 --> 00:45:28.820
or specific to the character you're playing as.

00:45:29.020 --> 00:45:33.100
So it's just like, oh, yeah, we have this piece of a crown that needs to be guarded.

00:45:33.260 --> 00:45:34.800
Might as well put a dragon here.

00:45:36.520 --> 00:45:37.560
Yeah, right, right.

00:45:37.780 --> 00:45:42.220
And it's funny, though, because doing it with full health, then, I guess, is the big difference.

00:45:42.380 --> 00:45:46.400
But my experience with this is, like, a boss battle is, like, a little five-second,

00:45:46.520 --> 00:45:50.760
literally five-second detour of just, like, kill the boss.

00:45:50.760 --> 00:45:54.080
And then it's, like, and they just fly back to the place or whatever.

00:45:55.340 --> 00:45:59.180
I don't know if later bosses maybe get more health or something,

00:45:59.220 --> 00:46:01.480
but I can't imagine that they have, like, boss patterns

00:46:01.480 --> 00:46:03.740
because the first two had no boss patterns at all.

00:46:03.920 --> 00:46:04.880
It was just, like, standing.

00:46:04.880 --> 00:46:06.020
They don't have any boss patterns.

00:46:06.320 --> 00:46:10.040
Yeah. All right, there's one thing I wanted to talk about that maybe we should have talked about

00:46:10.040 --> 00:46:12.960
at the beginning, but I feel like it's funny that we're going to mention it now

00:46:12.960 --> 00:46:14.840
after we've talked about so much of this game.

00:46:14.840 --> 00:46:20.160
But there's that moment probably within, like, the first five minutes of the game

00:46:20.160 --> 00:46:24.040
that I feel like my brain registered, but I didn't really register.

00:46:24.180 --> 00:46:28.000
And then as I was writing down notes for the game, I was like, oh, yeah, like, that was a thing.

00:46:28.360 --> 00:46:34.460
What did you guys think about when you first go down to the cavern, right, in the dungeon,

00:46:34.460 --> 00:46:36.760
and you find out that, like, that's all there is?

00:46:37.140 --> 00:46:40.400
It's just this – the whole game is just this cavern.

00:46:40.400 --> 00:46:45.900
And something about, like, leaving your house and going to the, like, the top part of the game

00:46:45.900 --> 00:46:51.660
where it's, like, the overworld and everything, I felt like I naturally stumbled into this cavern

00:46:51.660 --> 00:46:54.880
and thought, like, oh, I'll just take a detroit here and see what's down here.

00:46:55.040 --> 00:46:58.020
But the fact that, like, the whole game is underground

00:46:58.020 --> 00:47:02.260
and that there's not much happening in the overworld is just, like, an interesting thing, right?

00:47:02.280 --> 00:47:06.840
It's like a different – it's like a – oh, yeah, there could have been a whole other thing going on

00:47:06.840 --> 00:47:08.400
with this game above ground.

00:47:09.240 --> 00:47:14.680
Yeah, I mean, I actually tried to go to the right first, then I tried to go to the left,

00:47:14.720 --> 00:47:22.460
and then only when I realized that, like, the overworld of this game is just your house,

00:47:22.640 --> 00:47:26.080
it's like, okay, fine, I'll go into the catacombs or whatever.

00:47:26.800 --> 00:47:32.280
So, yeah, right off the bat, I realized that this is all there is.

00:47:33.120 --> 00:47:34.960
Yeah. Yeah, I was kind of the same way.

00:47:35.080 --> 00:47:36.820
I went to the – yeah, I went to the left.

00:47:37.100 --> 00:47:38.060
There was nothing there.

00:47:38.400 --> 00:47:42.060
I only skipped over that ladder because I was like, well, I don't feel like going into a dungeon just yet.

00:47:42.060 --> 00:47:43.420
I want to explore the lore a little bit.

00:47:43.540 --> 00:47:46.720
And I was like, oh, okay, I'll just go into this dungeon for the entire game.

00:47:47.120 --> 00:47:50.400
But I also don't think it's, like, a terrible – I think it's a cool idea.

00:47:50.580 --> 00:47:52.620
It's just one large dungeon.

00:47:52.740 --> 00:47:53.580
I don't think it's a bad thing.

00:47:53.640 --> 00:47:56.220
It just was, like you said, it was just, like, not expected at all.

00:48:25.900 --> 00:48:42.280
It's just one large dungeon. So just to get into sequels and spinoffs for a second, you know, I mentioned early on that this is actually a Dragon Slayer game.

00:48:42.640 --> 00:48:46.320
I don't think we got any of the Dragon Slayer games before this.

00:48:46.600 --> 00:48:54.080
So rather than just call it Dragon Slayer, which I guess could have made sense, but they didn't want to call Dragon Slayer 4, obviously.

00:48:54.080 --> 00:48:56.160
So it became Legacy of the Wizard.

00:48:56.760 --> 00:49:05.660
And it seems like in the Dragon Slayer, like, series of games, right, most Dragon Slayer games become, like, series of their own.

00:49:05.800 --> 00:49:07.940
Like, they get spinoffs and stuff.

00:49:08.160 --> 00:49:10.920
But Dragon Slayer 4 did not get any.

00:49:11.580 --> 00:49:15.700
Dragon Slayer 1 had, like, Dragon Slayer Gaiden released in 92.

00:49:16.140 --> 00:49:29.500
Dragon Slayer 2 is actually Xanadu, which we'll get to play as, I'm not sure if I'm going to say this right, but that famous NES game, Faxanadu, that everybody keeps saying, like, oh, if you guys love Zelda, you'll love Faxanadu.

00:49:30.880 --> 00:49:35.940
So, you know, we will experience more games in this Dragon Slayer series on the nostalgia exploration.

00:49:36.360 --> 00:49:42.460
But it's also kind of interesting that there's, like, this whole Dragon Slayer timeline that just is unfamiliar to me.

00:49:42.600 --> 00:49:44.340
It's like, again, I've heard of the series.

00:49:44.540 --> 00:49:53.720
But if you told me, like, you know, Sorcerian or Lord Monarch or Dragon Slayer Jr., I'm like, I've never heard of these games.

00:49:53.720 --> 00:49:55.940
Joe, Sean, anything ring a bell?

00:49:56.620 --> 00:49:59.280
You're just saying, like, syllables right now.

00:50:00.640 --> 00:50:02.200
Yeah, none of this at all.

00:50:02.300 --> 00:50:03.340
Even Faxanadu, though?

00:50:04.080 --> 00:50:05.220
Oh, Faxanadu, yes.

00:50:05.520 --> 00:50:07.940
I've never heard. Is that, like, F-A-C?

00:50:08.920 --> 00:50:10.220
Faxanadu? F-A-X.

00:50:11.720 --> 00:50:14.900
Faxanadu. Okay. So, I've only heard it.

00:50:14.960 --> 00:50:16.120
I recognize the name.

00:50:16.220 --> 00:50:20.020
And I actually just recently just kind of, like, picked it up for, like, a second.

00:50:20.200 --> 00:50:21.820
Played, like, the first, like, five minutes of it.

00:50:21.920 --> 00:50:23.700
You're playing ahead. It did look actually kind of interesting.

00:50:24.280 --> 00:50:27.960
Yeah, once in a while, I'll, you know, I'll check out an NES game, you know?

00:50:28.140 --> 00:50:29.160
You stop it right now.

00:50:29.660 --> 00:50:31.580
Oh, shit. I shouldn't have said that.

00:50:33.240 --> 00:50:42.120
I will say, though, just, like, from looking at these other Dragon Slayer games, they all seem to be side-scrolling action RPGs.

00:50:42.120 --> 00:50:50.140
So, I could only imagine that, like, you know, it's not like they would decline in quality as time goes on.

00:50:50.220 --> 00:50:51.800
They probably learned from the other ones.

00:50:51.860 --> 00:50:58.420
So, it makes me wonder, like, I would love to just go back and play the original Dragon Slayer in comparison to this.

00:50:58.420 --> 00:51:05.300
Because it's just essentially, like, an earlier version of this game that we have here, you know?

00:51:05.420 --> 00:51:06.720
Which just feels odd to say.

00:51:07.880 --> 00:51:09.960
An earlier version of an early game.

00:51:11.360 --> 00:51:16.320
And that's really all I have for Legacy of the Wizard, a.k.a.

00:51:16.640 --> 00:51:18.080
Dragon Slayer 4, a.k.a.

00:51:18.660 --> 00:51:22.080
Dragon Slayer part of the Xanadu series.

00:51:23.280 --> 00:51:32.660
But we have to figure out if, you know, this particular Dragon Slayer game will make it onto a list that we call the Essential Games List.

00:51:36.600 --> 00:51:39.700
Joe, last episode, I saved you for last.

00:51:39.880 --> 00:51:41.400
This episode, you're going to go first.

00:51:41.580 --> 00:51:45.340
What are your thoughts on Legacy of the Wizard?

00:51:46.220 --> 00:51:52.440
So, coming into this episode, I, in my head, was like, okay, I've got plenty of good things to say about this.

00:51:52.460 --> 00:51:59.420
And I still kind of, I still did, do think that this game has a lot of positives for it.

00:51:59.540 --> 00:52:06.720
But, man, doing this episode and, like, listing all the negatives out with you guys really made me realize, like, there's a lot more negatives than I was, like, thinking when I played the game.

00:52:06.940 --> 00:52:10.360
But it doesn't take away from the fact that I actually did enjoy it.

00:52:10.360 --> 00:52:23.220
Like, I think I just enjoyed the idea of, like, of having these characters and having, like, we talked about having the reasons to backtrack with them and find different items.

00:52:23.300 --> 00:52:24.580
And I think it was a really cool idea.

00:52:24.580 --> 00:52:31.660
And it was nice to have these characters that had significant differences that actually made them play differently.

00:52:33.400 --> 00:52:39.920
But, yeah, I mean, there are obviously more negatives to the gameplay that kind of outweigh those positives.

00:52:40.200 --> 00:52:45.540
But I don't think that would deter me from telling people to play it.

00:52:45.800 --> 00:52:49.420
But it definitely deters me from putting it on the Essential Games list.

00:52:49.520 --> 00:52:51.360
It's just not quite there.

00:52:51.820 --> 00:52:53.280
Sean, your thoughts?

00:52:54.420 --> 00:53:20.160
Yeah, this game has a lot of unique stuff going on between, like, just the size of it, the whole construction of how the game is played with multiple characters and a big branching dungeon and, like, the mishmash of gameplay elements that they've implemented here.

00:53:21.460 --> 00:53:25.780
I have a lot of problems with its execution, like we've been saying.

00:53:26.740 --> 00:53:39.980
So I think, yeah, if you're curious and want some kind of, like, RPG-lite platformer thing, I'd go ahead and give it a shot.

00:53:40.400 --> 00:53:43.880
But I can't put it on the Essential Games list either.

00:53:43.880 --> 00:53:55.420
I think another thing that we forgot to mention is most of the games that we've been playing in 1989 have been, you know, shorter experiences.

00:53:55.420 --> 00:54:00.100
A lot of them haven't, like, created tedious routes for us on purpose or anything like that.

00:54:00.100 --> 00:54:03.580
They've sort of learned from the mistakes of the earlier NES games.

00:54:03.580 --> 00:54:10.100
This one, like, goes back to a lot of – and maybe it's because it is a younger game that's just now getting ported.

00:54:10.400 --> 00:54:18.200
But there's just a lot of things here that could make this, like, dozens of hours of your time if you play without a guide.

00:54:18.320 --> 00:54:24.120
You know, like, I really can't imagine somebody even coming close to beating this game without some help.

00:54:24.500 --> 00:54:29.740
You know, who knows what those children did in 1989 to play this game and beat it.

00:54:30.100 --> 00:54:31.500
You know, I – Think of the children.

00:54:31.500 --> 00:54:38.420
No, I'm just saying, you know, it's like, did it even happen or did they just, like – was the fun just seeing how far you could get?

00:54:38.580 --> 00:54:41.260
Like, it feels weird to say that because I don't think that would be fun.

00:54:41.300 --> 00:54:44.420
And that's why, for me, it's not going to be on the Essential Games list.

00:54:44.600 --> 00:54:49.620
It's a gigantic game with very little reason to continue to push forward.

00:54:50.800 --> 00:54:55.800
And I think under different circumstances, maybe the characters could have been more unique.

00:54:55.800 --> 00:54:59.380
Maybe the map could have been more unique in terms of working with the characters.

00:54:59.380 --> 00:55:09.520
See how, like, there's this theme of uniqueness because that's what gets you on the Essential Games list, standing out and doing something that the rest of the pack doesn't do.

00:55:10.280 --> 00:55:13.880
And unfortunately for this game, I don't see anything here that represents that.

00:55:13.880 --> 00:55:18.160
So it's not an Essential Game, you know, but we've had a lot.

00:55:18.160 --> 00:55:22.900
We've had a lot of games so far in 1989 become Essential Games.

00:55:22.920 --> 00:55:25.200
So I'm not feeling a loss here.

00:55:25.240 --> 00:55:29.120
Are you guys? I feel great shame.

00:55:31.280 --> 00:55:37.340
I'm just saying, in 23 episodes in 1989, we already have three Essential Games.

00:55:37.760 --> 00:55:41.400
That's, you know, that's like every 10 games we get an Essential.

00:55:41.980 --> 00:55:46.280
Yeah, and Essentials that we weren't expecting, you know, Essentials that we had never heard of.

00:55:46.680 --> 00:55:48.420
Absolutely. That's cool.

00:55:48.660 --> 00:55:53.760
Yeah. We also have next week another potential Essential game.

00:55:53.960 --> 00:55:58.120
You could technically say the following 600 games could all be Essential.

00:55:58.580 --> 00:56:01.460
So who's, you know, we won't know until we play them.

00:56:01.620 --> 00:56:03.580
So next week we have Mappy Land.

00:56:04.040 --> 00:56:10.920
It sounds like a game for your Garmin, but we're going to play it.

00:56:11.080 --> 00:56:14.000
And Joe, any guesses on Mappy Land?

00:56:14.680 --> 00:56:16.000
Well, this is another one.

00:56:16.120 --> 00:56:17.980
You know, maybe I know this one.

00:56:18.140 --> 00:56:19.040
I know of Mappy Land.

00:56:19.240 --> 00:56:21.280
Oh, okay. Did you play the entire game before?

00:56:22.020 --> 00:56:23.860
Yeah, I love Mappy Land.

00:56:24.380 --> 00:56:26.680
I think I've only watched the game Grumps play it.

00:56:26.700 --> 00:56:29.440
I don't think I ever actually played it myself, but I do know what it is.

00:56:29.520 --> 00:56:30.680
I won't spoil it for you.

00:56:30.780 --> 00:56:35.680
That's fine. Sean, the episode after that is Mystery Quest.

00:56:35.980 --> 00:56:38.840
Any ideas what the mystery in that quest is?

00:56:41.760 --> 00:56:48.380
Um, I'm assuming it's going to be like, uh, um, actually no.

00:56:48.380 --> 00:56:50.380
I have nothing clever to say.

00:56:50.380 --> 00:56:53.280
I also have no idea what it could actually be.

00:56:53.680 --> 00:56:58.140
Well, yeah, you're definitely going to go on a quest, but of what kind is a mystery?

00:56:59.080 --> 00:57:00.280
Oh, there it is.

00:57:00.320 --> 00:57:01.780
I could have wrote the back of the box for them.

00:57:03.020 --> 00:57:05.940
You're definitely going to go on a quest, but...

00:57:05.940 --> 00:57:13.100
And the last episode we'll go into for future teases here is Predator, which I can only imagine

00:57:13.100 --> 00:57:18.060
is a game based on the hit TV show to catch a predator with Chris Hansen.

00:57:18.760 --> 00:57:21.580
So I'm happy to go catch those kids.

00:57:22.180 --> 00:57:23.940
Not the kids. No, you catch the predators.

00:57:24.180 --> 00:57:26.060
Yeah, yeah, yeah. I forgot the point of the show.

00:57:26.200 --> 00:57:28.220
I forget the word. It's in the title.

00:57:28.420 --> 00:57:31.980
Yeah, I forget, like, you know, who's really on whose side in those shows.

00:57:33.360 --> 00:57:34.360
That was good.

00:57:34.360 --> 00:57:36.660
I like that. Anyway, yeah, yeah, that's good.

00:57:36.820 --> 00:57:37.840
I bet you that that's good.

00:57:38.540 --> 00:57:41.220
Why don't you sit down and have a seat, Sean, all right?

00:57:42.740 --> 00:57:44.180
Michael Esposito, Nostalgia.

00:57:44.180 --> 00:58:09.300
Outro Music
